1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Hi, I'm Anna. I designed Evergreen and our little pine buddy.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,000
Most of us want to work out a bit more than our normal.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,500
But a plan breaks the moment it meets a real calendar.

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,000
A meeting runs late, it rains, your shoes wear out, and the week slips.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,500
Evergreen is a personal training agent that works inside your real life.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,500
It reads Apple Health: 21 runs in 30 days, an average heart rate of 132,

7
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,500
and a training load that's up 19% on your usual week.

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Then it reads your calendar and finds the time you actually have.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,500
This week: Friday evening, and Saturday and Sunday mornings.

10
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,000
Three workouts, each with a reason you can see.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Jonathan, show them what it does.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,500
Thanks, Anna. I'm Jonathan, and I built the iOS app.

13
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,500
Evergreen doesn't just plan. It acts for you, end to end.

14
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:57,000
At 4 PM, a team sync lands right on top of tonight's 6 PM run.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,500
Nobody taps anything. The agent sees the conflict and moves the run to 6:45,

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:06,000
the next open slot, so the week still has all three workouts.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,500
It tells you why, and Undo is one tap away.

18
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Now rain is forecast for Saturday's 8:30 AM long run.

19
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,500
It finds a $15 day pass at 24 Hour Fitness, 0.4 miles from home.

20
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:23,500
Spending is where it always asks first. Nothing is bought without your OK.

21
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:27,500
One tap, and it books the pass and moves the run onto a treadmill.

22
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Surya, show them how it shops.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,500
Thanks, Jonathan. I'm Surya. I built the recommendation harness.

24
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:38,000
Jordan's shoes are at 392 miles, and his race is in nine days.

25
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,500
Our harness on Vercel uses Nimble to research real shoes on expert review sites.

26
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,000
Hard rules run in code: wide toe box, no carbon plate, arrives in time to break in.

27
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,500
Then Superlinked's SIE ranks the shortlist with a Qwen reranker,

28
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:56,000
and a Qwen model picks one and writes why every other shoe lost.

29
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,500
Senso supplies cited passages, live, so every claim has a source.

30
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:05,000
Every recommendation is written to a SingleStore audit log.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,500
If anything fails, guardrails fall back to a safe, deterministic pick.

32
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,000
Jordan says yes once, and two break-in runs land on his calendar.

33
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Tony, take us home.

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,500
Thanks, Surya. I'm Tony. I built the data and the memory agent.

35
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:23,000
Evergreen remembers you, on your phone. The memory agent runs in JavaScriptCore.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,500
Every tick, it rolls your history into a memory of how you actually train.

37
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:32,000
Workouts, memory updates and approved purchases go to Amplitude,

38
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,500
with opaque, hashed ids. No names or health readings leave the phone.

39
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:41,000
So we see what the agent does and what people say yes to, not who they are.

40
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,500
Evergreen plans around your real life, acts on its own, and asks before it spends.

41
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,000
Training that grows with you.

42
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
We're Anna, Jonathan, Surya and Tony. Thank you!

